---
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';
import LineChart from '../components/LineChart.astro';
import v01 from '../../public/data/runs/p1_01_forward_validation.json';
import tt from '../../public/data/runs/p1_02_traveltime.json';
import sc3 from '../../public/data/runs/p1_03_scattered.json';
import am from '../../public/data/runs/p1_04_ambient.json';
import fw from '../../public/data/runs/p1_05_fwi.json';
import SectionMap from '../components/SectionMap.astro';
import Scoreboard from '../components/Scoreboard.astro';
import RunDetails from '../components/RunDetails.astro';
import Term from '../components/Term.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const st = v01.stokes;
const ra = v01.rayleigh;
const rc = v01.reciprocity;
const ab = v01.absorbing;
const run = v01.manifest;
const ray10 = ra.rows.find((r) => r.points_per_wavelength === 10)!;
const cx = tt.surveys.crosshole;
const su = tt.surveys.surface;
const chamber = { x0: -3, x1: 3, y0: -18, y1: -12 };
const holesXZ = (cx.stations.receivers as number[][]).filter((p) => Math.abs(p[1]) < 0.3).map((p) => [p[0], p[2]] as [number, number]);
const surfXZ = (su.stations.receivers as number[][]).filter((p) => Math.abs(p[1]) < 0.3).map((p) => [p[0], p[2]] as [number, number]);
const pct = (v: number) => `${v > 0 ? '+' : ''}${(v * 100).toFixed(1)}%`;
const e3 = sc3.score;
const surfXY = (sc3.survey.stations.receivers as number[][]).map((p) => [p[0], p[1]] as [number, number]);
const a4 = am.score;
const rt = am.retrieval;
const cvr = a4.contrast_vs_record;
const needS = a4.record_for_contrast_3_s;
const fs = fw.score;
const fst = fw.stages[0];
const fh = fst.history;
const fwSurfXZ = (fw.stations.receivers as number[][]).filter((p) => Math.abs(p[1]) < 0.3).map((p) => [p[0], p[2]] as [number, number]);
const dur = (s: number) => (s < 60 ? `${s.toFixed(0)} s` : s < 3600 ? `${(s / 60).toFixed(0)} min` : s < 86400 ? `${(s / 3600).toFixed(s < 36000 ? 1 : 0)} h` : `${(s / 86400).toFixed(0)} days`);
---
<Doc
  title="Geophones: what instruments on the ground see"
  kicker="From the ground · what the right instruments see"
  current="seismic"
  lede="Before judging a satellite, ask what the instruments built for the job can see. Geophysicists image the shallow ground with arrays of geophones and a source, hammer blows or the ambient hum, and invert the records into a three-dimensional tomogram. Here that is done in simulation, over the same ground the satellite was flown over. Boreholes, echo imaging and full-waveform inversion find the chamber; the lab’s tools find it when the physics allows, so the satellite’s failure is the satellite’s."
  state="status · solver validated · travel times, echo imaging, ambient noise and full-waveform inversion done on the one-chamber bench"
  index={[
    { id: 'chain', label: 'The chain' },
    { id: 'scoreboard', label: 'Scoreboard' },
    { id: 'forward', label: 'Forward model' },
    { id: 'traveltime', label: 'Travel times' },
    { id: 'echo', label: 'Echo imaging' },
    { id: 'ambient', label: 'Ambient noise' },
    { id: 'fwi', label: 'Full waveform' },
    { id: 'arrays', label: 'Arrays' },
    { id: 'methods', label: 'Three methods' },
    { id: 'scoring', label: 'Scoring' },
  ]}
>
  <section id="scoreboard">
    <h2>Where each method stands on the one-chamber bench</h2>
    <Scoreboard />
  </section>

  <section id="chain">
    <h2>Every tomogram here is scored against a truth the method never saw</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The ground is composed first, from sourced materials. The waves are computed through it by a solver that knows
      nothing about any inversion. The inversions see only what the sensors recorded, with noise, on a different grid.
      Whatever they draw is then compared with the chambers that are really there.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">compose</span>
        <h3>Known ground</h3>
        <p>
          Test benches from one chamber to the claimed underworld, and Giza. Strata, sand cover, water table and von
          Kármán heterogeneity, all as data. <a href={`${base}/underworld/`}>See them in the underworld.</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">simulate</span>
        <h3>Waves through it</h3>
        <p>3-D isotropic elastic velocity–stress finite differences, fourth order in space, absorbing boundaries on the sides and floor, a free surface on top, air-filled chambers as traction-free cavities.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">recover</span>
        <h3>What practitioners would draw</h3>
        <p>Travel-time tomography, ambient-noise tomography and full-waveform inversion, each run as it would be run in the field, then scored voxel by voxel.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="forward">
    <h2>The solver matches exact solutions to within a fraction of a percent</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Before it touches a chamber, the forward model is checked where the answer is known exactly, in Mokattam limestone
      (P {v01.manifest.params.vp_m_s.toLocaleString('en-US')} m/s, S {v01.manifest.params.vs_m_s.toLocaleString('en-US')} m/s).
    </p>
    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{st.worst_ncc.toFixed(3)}</span><span class="what">worst correlation with the exact point-force response, every component</span><span class="src">Stokes · {st.points_per_wavelength.toFixed(0)} points per wavelength</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{(100 * st.worst_amplitude_error).toFixed(1)}<small>%</small></span><span class="what">worst amplitude error against the same solution</span><span class="src">Stokes</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{ray10.error_percent.toFixed(2)}<small>%</small></span><span class="what">Rayleigh-wave speed error on the free surface at ten points per S wavelength</span><span class="src">Rayleigh equation</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{rc.ncc.toFixed(4)}</span><span class="what">reciprocity: swapping source and receiver across a {rc.void_m} m void</span><span class="src">reciprocity</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{ab.final_relative.toExponential(0).replace('e-', '×10⁻').replace(/(\d+)$/, (d) => [...d].map((c) => '⁰¹²³⁴⁵⁶⁷⁸⁹'[+c]).join(''))}</span><span class="what">of the peak energy left in the grid once the waves have gone</span><span class="src">absorbing boundaries</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      {
        st.panels.map((p, i) => (
          <LineChart
            title={`Fig. 1${'abcd'[i]} · Point force: ${p.component} velocity, ${p.receiver}, ${p.offset_m.toFixed(0)} m`}
            series={[
              { name: 'exact', x: p.t_ms, y: p.exact, color: 'var(--text)', width: 1.5 },
              { name: 'simulated', x: p.t_ms, y: p.simulated, color: 'var(--verdigris)', dash: '4 3' },
            ]}
            xLabel="time, ms"
            yLabel="velocity (scaled)"
            height={170}
            zeroLine
            directLabels={false}
            caption={`correlation ${p.ncc.toFixed(4)} · amplitude ratio ${p.amplitude_ratio.toFixed(3)}`}
          />
        ))
      }
      <LineChart
        title="Fig. 2 · Rayleigh-wave speed on the free surface"
        series={[{ name: 'error', x: ra.rows.map((r) => r.points_per_wavelength), y: ra.rows.map((r) => r.error_percent), color: 'var(--verdigris)', markers: true }]}
        xLabel="points per minimum S wavelength"
        yLabel="speed error"
        yFormat="pct"
        height={170}
        zeroLine
        yDomain={[-1.2, 0.2]}
        caption={`measured by cross-correlation at 3 to 8 wavelengths; exact ${ra.exact_m_s.toFixed(0)} m/s from the Rayleigh equation`}
      />
      <LineChart
        title="Fig. 3 · Energy left in the grid"
        series={[{ name: 'energy', x: ab.t_ms, y: ab.energy_relative, color: 'var(--verdigris)' }]}
        xLabel="time, ms"
        yLabel="relative energy"
        logY
        yFormat="exp"
        height={170}
        yDomain={[1e-10, 1]}
        caption={`C-PML, ${ab.pml_cells} cells on every face`}
      />
      <LineChart
        title={`Fig. 4 · Reciprocity across a ${rc.void_m} m void`}
        series={[
          { name: 'east force → up velocity', x: rc.t_ms, y: rc.forward, color: 'var(--text)', width: 1.5 },
          { name: 'up force → east velocity', x: rc.t_ms, y: rc.reverse, color: 'var(--verdigris)', dash: '4 3' },
        ]}
        xLabel="time, ms"
        yLabel="velocity (scaled)"
        height={170}
        zeroLine
        directLabels={false}
        caption={`correlation ${rc.ncc.toFixed(5)} · amplitude ratio ${rc.amplitude_ratio.toFixed(3)}`}
      />
    </div>
    <RunDetails manifest={run} extra={[['grid', `${st.grid.join(' × ')} cells at ${st.spacing_m} m`], ['points per S wavelength', st.points_per_wavelength.toFixed(1)], ['speed', `${st.mcell_steps_per_s.toFixed(0)} M cell-steps per second`]]} />
  </section>

  <section id="traveltime">
    <h2>Travel times catch the chamber's shadow from boreholes, and nothing from the surface</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The one-chamber bench: a 6 m air-filled cube, its top 12 m down, in bare limestone. Every record comes from the
      solver above, with noise 30 dB below each record's peak; <Term k="first-break">first arrivals</Term> are picked by waveform correlation; the picks are
      inverted on a 1 m grid by a code that never sees the model. Air is {Math.round(-100 * cx.score.true_air_rel)}% slower
      than the rock; a first arrival goes around the void instead of through it, so what travel times can see is the
      delay of the detour.
    </p>
    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{pct(cx.score.chamber_mean_rel)}</span><span class="what">speed recovered inside the chamber from four boreholes</span><span class="src">crosshole · {cx.sources} shots, {cx.receivers} sensors</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{cx.score.contrast_to_noise.toFixed(1)}<small>×</small></span><span class="what">the chamber's anomaly against the tomogram's own scatter around it</span><span class="src">crosshole</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{pct(su.score.chamber_mean_rel)}</span><span class="what">speed recovered inside the chamber from the surface</span><span class="src">surface · {su.sources} shots, {su.receivers} geophones</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{(cx.pick_minus_straight_ms.p95_abs).toFixed(2)}<small>ms</small></span><span class="what">95th-percentile pick error against straight rays, the precision the picks carry</span><span class="src">crosshole picks</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="grid-3 charts">
      <SectionMap
        title="Fig. 5a · Crosshole: vertical section through the chamber"
        x={cx.slices.x}
        y={cx.slices.z}
        values={cx.slices.xz_rel}
        domain={0.08}
        outlines={[chamber]}
        points={holesXZ}
        xLabel="east, m"
        yLabel="height, m"
        caption={`relative P speed · sparker at ${tt.manifest.params.f0_hz.crosshole} Hz · boreholes at ±12 m`}
      />
      <SectionMap
        title="Fig. 5b · Crosshole: horizontal section at the chamber's centre"
        x={cx.slices.x}
        y={cx.slices.y.slice().reverse()}
        values={cx.slices.xy_rel.slice().reverse()}
        domain={0.08}
        outlines={[{ x0: -3, x1: 3, y0: -3, y1: 3 }]}
        points={[[-12, 0], [12, 0], [0, -12], [0, 12]]}
        xLabel="east, m"
        yLabel="north, m"
        caption="the four boreholes in ochre"
      />
      <SectionMap
        title="Fig. 5c · Surface: vertical section through the chamber"
        x={su.slices.x}
        y={su.slices.z}
        values={su.slices.xz_rel}
        domain={0.08}
        outlines={[chamber]}
        points={surfXZ}
        xLabel="east, m"
        yLabel="height, m"
        caption={`hammer at ${tt.manifest.params.f0_hz.surface} Hz · the weak direct P picked on 16 stacked blows (${Math.round(su.starting_speed_m_s).toLocaleString('en-US')} m/s) · in uniform rock no first-arrival path reaches 12 m`}
      />
    </div>
    <p class="finding">{tt.finding}</p>
    <RunDetails manifest={tt.manifest} extra={[['crosshole picks', `${cx.picked} of ${cx.pairs} pairs · 95th-percentile error ${cx.pick_minus_straight_ms.p95_abs.toFixed(2)} ms`], ['surface picks', `${su.picked} of ${su.pairs} pairs after quality control against the survey's own pick spread (at short offsets the surface wave arrives within a quarter period of the P) · noise 50 dB below each peak (16 stacked blows at a quiet site; at 30 dB the direct P is lost under the surface wave)`], ['inversion', `Gauss-Newton, ${cx.history.length - 1} iterations · smoothing 3 · damping 0.05 · 1 m cells`], ['picking', 'correlation with the leading lobe of the far-field P pulse'], ['viewer', 'the tomograms are in the underworld: bench-void, Show: Recovered']]} />
  </section>

  <section id="echo">
    <h2>The chamber's echo, too faint to see on any record, images it from the surface</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The same hammer and geophone grids that saw nothing by travel times. Records are made twice, with and without the
      chamber; the difference is its echo. On a typical record that echo is {Math.abs(e3.echo_over_record_db).toFixed(0)} dB
      below the waves and {Math.abs(e3.echo_over_noise_db).toFixed(0)} dB below the noise. Sent back through the rock by{' '}
      <Term k="rtm">reverse-time migration</Term>, 25 shots and 81 geophones add up where the echo came from. The rock
      without the chamber is known exactly here, so this is the most favourable case for any waveform method: the first
      step of <Term k="fwi">full-waveform inversion</Term>, taken with a perfect starting model.
    </p>
    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{e3.echo_over_record_db.toFixed(0)}<small>dB</small></span><span class="what">the chamber's echo against the whole record</span><span class="src">median over 2,025 records</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{e3.echo_over_noise_db.toFixed(0)}<small>dB</small></span><span class="what">the echo against the noise on the same record</span><span class="src">noise 30 dB below each peak</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{e3.contrast.toFixed(1)}<small>×</small></span><span class="what">the migrated image at the chamber against the image elsewhere</span><span class="src">below 3 m, 2 m around the chamber</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{e3.brightest_offset_from_chamber_m.toFixed(1)}<small>m</small></span><span class="what">from the chamber's centre to the brightest point, on its top face</span><span class="src">image below 3 m</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <SectionMap
        title="Fig. 6a · Echo image: vertical section through the chamber"
        x={sc3.slices.x}
        y={sc3.slices.z}
        values={sc3.slices.xz_image}
        domain={0.6}
        scale="sequential"
        outlines={[{ x0: -3, x1: 3, y0: -18, y1: -12 }]}
        xLabel="east, m"
        yLabel="height, m"
        width={360}
        caption="migrated echo amplitude, normalised · strongest at the chamber's top face, where the waves turn back"
      />
      <SectionMap
        title="Fig. 6b · Echo image: horizontal section at the chamber's centre"
        x={sc3.slices.x}
        y={sc3.slices.y.slice().reverse()}
        values={sc3.slices.xy_image.slice().reverse()}
        domain={0.6}
        scale="sequential"
        outlines={[{ x0: -3, x1: 3, y0: -3, y1: 3 }]}
        points={surfXY}
        xLabel="east, m"
        yLabel="north, m"
        width={360}
        caption="at the chamber's centre depth; geophones 4 m apart in ochre, those over the imaged area"
      />
    </div>
    <p class="finding">{sc3.finding}</p>
    <RunDetails manifest={sc3.manifest} extra={[['survey', sc3.survey.description], ['imaging', 'reverse-time migration, velocity cross-correlation, source-illumination compensation, Laplacian filter'], ['background', 'exact: the site without its chamber (the most favourable case)'], ['viewer', 'Show: Recovered (echo imaging) and Show: Waves (the hammer blow; the echo alone)']]} />
  </section>

  <section id="ambient">
    <h2>Noise stands in for the hammer, but its image of the chamber tops out at {a4.image_contrast_ensemble.toFixed(1)}×, however long it listens</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      No hammer: {am.manifest.params.noise_sources} noise sources scattered over the ground around the same geophone grid,
      each a stream of random vertical pushes, like footsteps and traffic. Cross-correlating the noise at two geophones
      gives the wave one would send the other (<Term k="ambient-noise">interferometry</Term>), so every geophone becomes a{' '}
      <Term k="virtual-source">virtual source</Term>. {am.manifest.params.virtual_sources} of them are used as in echo
      imaging: the gathers predicted without the chamber are subtracted and what remains is migrated. The rock is known
      exactly and the noise field is averaged over an unlimited record, the most favourable passive case; a finite record
      adds fluctuations whose image falls as one over the square root of its length.
    </p>
    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{rt.ncc.toFixed(2)}</span><span class="what">correlation of the wave retrieved from noise with a real source's, {rt.distance_m.toFixed(0)} m apart</span><span class="src">minus the lag derivative of the noise correlation</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{a4.image_contrast_ensemble.toFixed(1)}<small>×</small></span><span class="what">the passive image at the chamber against the image elsewhere, with an unlimited record</span><span class="src">active echo imaging: {e3.contrast.toFixed(1)}×</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{needS ? dur(needS) : 'never'}</span><span class="what">of noise for the chamber to stand at 3× the rest of the image</span><span class="src">fluctuations scaled from a {a4.reference_record_s.toFixed(0)} s realisation</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{a4.brightest_offset_from_chamber_m.toFixed(1)}<small>m</small></span><span class="what">from the chamber's centre to the brightest point of the passive image</span><span class="src">image below 3 m</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="charts wide">
      <LineChart
        title={`Fig. 7a · The wave between two geophones ${rt.distance_m.toFixed(0)} m apart`}
        series={[
          { name: 'real source', x: rt.t_ms, y: rt.direct, color: 'var(--text)', width: 1.5 },
          { name: 'from noise', x: rt.t_ms, y: rt.correlation, color: 'var(--verdigris)', dash: '4 3' },
        ]}
        xLabel="time, ms"
        yLabel="vertical velocity (scaled)"
        height={160}
        zeroLine
        directLabels={false}
        caption={`correlation ${rt.ncc.toFixed(2)} · the noise correlation's zero lag placed where the real source fires`}
      />
    </div>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <SectionMap
        title="Fig. 7b · Passive echo image: vertical section through the chamber"
        x={am.slices.x}
        y={am.slices.z}
        values={am.slices.xz_image}
        domain={0.6}
        scale="sequential"
        outlines={[{ x0: -3, x1: 3, y0: -18, y1: -12 }]}
        xLabel="east, m"
        yLabel="height, m"
        width={330}
        caption="migrated passive echo amplitude, normalised · unlimited record, rock known exactly"
      />
      <LineChart
        title="Fig. 7c · Contrast against the length of the record"
        series={[
          { name: 'passive', x: cvr.map((r) => r.record_s), y: cvr.map((r) => r.contrast), color: 'var(--verdigris)', markers: true },
          { name: '3×', x: [cvr[0].record_s, cvr[cvr.length - 1].record_s], y: [3, 3], color: 'var(--faint)', dash: '3 3', width: 1 },
        ]}
        xLabel="noise recorded"
        yLabel="chamber / elsewhere"
        logX
        xFormat="duration"
        xTicks={[1, 60, 3600, 86400]}
        height={190}
        caption="each image's fluctuations scaled from one realisation: migration is linear, their variance falls as 1/T"
      />
    </div>
    <p class="finding">{am.finding}</p>
    <RunDetails manifest={am.manifest} extra={[['noise', `${am.manifest.params.noise_sources} independent band-limited vertical force streams around ${am.manifest.params.noise_band_centre_hz} Hz, ${am.manifest.params.noise_source_area}`], ['retrieval', 'ensemble correlations computed exactly from each source\u2019s response; the virtual wave is minus their lag derivative (Snieder 2004)'], ['imaging', `${am.manifest.params.virtual_sources} virtual sources, reverse-time migration as in echo imaging, one data scale for every image so they add linearly`], ['record length', `a finite record modelled as random source phases over ${am.manifest.params.fluctuation_reference_segments} segments of ${(2 * am.manifest.params.response_length_s).toFixed(3)} s`], ['viewer', 'Show: Recovered (ambient noise, passive)']]} />
  </section>

  <section id="fwi">
    <h2>Fitting whole records from a practitioner’s start finds the chamber as a {pct(fs.vs.chamber_mean_rel).replace('-', '−')} drop in S speed, {fs.vs.contrast_to_noise.toFixed(1)}× the scatter</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The same noisy surface records echo imaging migrated, now fitted whole by <Term k="fwi">full-waveform inversion</Term>.
      Unlike echo imaging, the rock is not given: the start is what a practitioner would have, uniform rock at the P speed
      the surface travel times gave ({Math.round(fw.manifest.params.start.vp_m_s).toLocaleString('en-US')} m/s) and an S speed from
      the textbook ratio √3 ({Math.round(fw.manifest.params.start.vs_m_s).toLocaleString('en-US')} m/s, 3% fast). Gradients of P and
      S speed come from correlating forward and adjoint stresses, checked against finite differences; traces within 4 m of
      their hammer are left out, and the iterations stop at the noise level or after ten; here after ten, just above it. The records were made with the
      same solver and grid the inversion uses (the “inverse crime”): the most favourable case for FWI, softened only by the
      noise and the wrong start.
    </p>
    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{pct(fs.vs.chamber_mean_rel)}</span><span class="what">S speed recovered inside the chamber</span><span class="src">{fs.vs.contrast_to_noise.toFixed(1)}× the scatter around it</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{pct(fs.vp.chamber_mean_rel)}</span><span class="what">P speed recovered inside the chamber</span><span class="src">{fs.vp.contrast_to_noise.toFixed(1)}× the scatter</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{Math.round(fs.vs.background_m_s).toLocaleString('en-US')}<small>m/s</small></span><span class="what">background S speed after inversion, from {Math.round(fw.manifest.params.start.vs_m_s).toLocaleString('en-US')}</span><span class="src">true 1,830 m/s</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{fh[fh.length - 1].misfit.toFixed(2)}</span><span class="what">misfit left after {fh.length - 1} iterations, where the noise alone leaves {fst.noise_misfit.toFixed(2)}</span><span class="src">{fst.traces_kept.toLocaleString('en-US')} of {fst.traces.toLocaleString('en-US')} traces</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="grid-3 charts">
      <SectionMap
        title="Fig. 8a · FWI, S speed: vertical section through the chamber"
        x={fw.slices.x}
        y={fw.slices.z}
        values={fw.slices.xz_vs}
        domain={0.15}
        outlines={[chamber]}
        points={fwSurfXZ}
        xLabel="east, m"
        yLabel="height, m"
        caption="relative S speed against the inverted background"
      />
      <SectionMap
        title="Fig. 8b · FWI, P speed: vertical section through the chamber"
        x={fw.slices.x}
        y={fw.slices.z}
        values={fw.slices.xz_vp}
        domain={0.15}
        outlines={[chamber]}
        points={fwSurfXZ}
        xLabel="east, m"
        yLabel="height, m"
        caption="relative P speed; for records dominated by the surface wave the P speed is the weaker constraint"
      />
      <LineChart
        title="Fig. 8c · Misfit against iteration"
        series={[
          { name: 'misfit', x: fh.map((h) => h.iteration), y: fh.map((h) => h.misfit), color: 'var(--verdigris)', markers: true },
          { name: 'noise level', x: [0, fh[fh.length - 1].iteration], y: [fst.noise_misfit, fst.noise_misfit], color: 'var(--faint)', dash: '3 3', width: 1 },
        ]}
        xLabel="iteration"
        yLabel="misfit / start"
        yDomain={[0, 1.05]}
        height={210}
        caption="L-BFGS with an illumination preconditioner; the noise level estimated from each record's quiet first 2 ms"
      />
    </div>
    <p class="finding">From uniform rock at the travel-time P speed and a textbook S speed, ten iterations fitting the whole records bring the misfit to {fh[fh.length - 1].misfit.toFixed(2)} of its start, against {fst.noise_misfit.toFixed(2)} for the noise alone. The chamber comes back as {pct(fs.vs.chamber_mean_rel)} in S speed, {fs.vs.contrast_to_noise.toFixed(1)}× the scatter around it, its slowest cell {fs.vs.slowest_offset_from_chamber_m.toFixed(1)} m from the chamber's centre, in its upper half; in P speed only {pct(fs.vp.chamber_mean_rel)}. The background S speed moves from {Math.round(fw.manifest.params.start.vs_m_s).toLocaleString('en-US')} to {Math.round(fs.vs.background_m_s).toLocaleString('en-US')} m/s, toward the true 1,830. Of the surface methods, this is the only one to put the chamber where it is with the rock not given, in the most favourable case.</p>
    <RunDetails manifest={fw.manifest} extra={[['start', 'uniform rock: Vp from P1-02 surface travel times, Vs = Vp/√3'], ['misfit', fw.manifest.params.misfit], ['optimiser', fw.manifest.params.optimiser], ['inverse crime', fw.manifest.params.inverse_crime], ['viewer', 'Show: Recovered (full-waveform inversion, S or P speed)']]} />
  </section>

  <section id="arrays">
    <h2>The arrays are the ones near-surface surveys actually deploy</h2>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Active</h3>
        <p>A grid of three-component geophones at 1 to 4 m spacing and a source walked across it (hammer or weight drop), with realistic source repeatability and sensor self-noise.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Passive</h3>
        <p>A dense nodal array recording the ambient hum of traffic, wind and visitors. It is the seismic cousin of the radar claim: natural vibration asked to reveal what lies below.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="methods">
    <h2>Three inversions, from the classic to the state of the art</h2>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3><Term k="traveltime">Travel-time tomography</Term></h3>
        <p>First arrivals picked from the records, bent rays traced through an eikonal solver, a regularised least-squares update. Robust, and blurred at a wavelength.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3><Term k="ambient-noise">Ambient-noise tomography</Term></h3>
        <p>Noise cross-correlated between sensor pairs becomes the wave that would travel between them. In practice its surface-wave dispersion is inverted for shear speed with depth; here the correlations serve as <Term k="virtual-source">virtual sources</Term> for echo imaging, the most a passive array can do.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3><Term k="fwi">Full-waveform inversion</Term></h3>
        <p>The whole record fitted by adjoint-state gradients, from low frequencies up. The sharpest images near-surface geophysics makes, and the most demanding.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scoring">
    <h2>Detectability is a map over size, depth and array, not a yes or no</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      For each method and each chamber: the contrast recovered at the chamber against the spread of the background, the
      error in position and size, and the false chambers drawn elsewhere. Swept over chamber size, depth, array aperture,
      spacing and frequency, the result would be a map of where seismology succeeds and where it stops. The satellite, judged
      on the same ground, is beyond that map’s edge (<a href={`${base}/satellite/`}>the satellite</a>).
    </p>
  </section>
